From b0ec2e36773fafc33f327f6977c8370068d76e51 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 19:54:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] Stop the timeline from desyncing lip-synced shots
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A lip-synced shot's frames are married to a specific slice of the
narration. Four places in the compositor move a clip's frames relative
to the timeline, all of them correct for b-roll and all of them
silently fatal here: none throws, none appears in a log, and each
produces a file that plays perfectly while the mouth says something
else. SceneSegment.lipSynced marks the shots that must not be moved.

SEEKING. compose.ts spreads a golden-ratio offset across scenes so
reused footage does not feel looped. Providers return discrete clip
lengths, so a 3s scene almost always has spare time and almost always
gets seeked — every synced shot would open mid-word. This is the one
that would have bitten first; the end-to-end test pins it with a
three-colour clip, and without the guard the shot opens on the wrong
colour.

LOOPING. A clip shorter than its scene got -stream_loop -1, which
replays the mouth mid-sentence. Synced shots now hold their last frame
(tpad), the same choice enforceClipDuration already makes and for the
same reason.

DIVERSIFYING. diversifySceneClips reassigns clipIndex when adjacent
scenes repeat. With a cast, that puts one character's mouth under
another character's words. Locked in both directions now: a synced
scene keeps its clip, and no other scene may be handed that clip.

SPLITTING. resplitLongHolds cuts a long hold into pieces with a
rotating clipIndex — halving a performance and swapping the face
mid-line. Synced scenes pass through whole, and splits elsewhere never
rotate onto footage carrying a performance. A long take of someone
talking is not the problem that pass exists to fix.

Behaviour for scenes without the flag is unchanged; the existing
planGate tests still pass untouched.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
